WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.097
Approval should bind a human’s decision to an exact action: account, resource,

00:00:06.097 --> 00:00:09.146
amount, parameters, expiry, and unique identifier.

00:00:09.296 --> 00:00:13.437
A generic yes in a conversation is ambiguous when proposals change.

00:00:13.587 --> 00:00:17.619
Store the proposal server-side and compare the approved version with the stored

00:00:17.619 --> 00:00:17.955
version.

00:00:18.105 --> 00:00:21.979
Recheck current business eligibility immediately before execution.

00:00:22.129 --> 00:00:23.518
Retries are inevitable.

00:00:23.668 --> 00:00:28.097
Use a stable idempotency key for one logical effect and persist the

00:00:28.097 --> 00:00:29.942
effect record atomically where possible.

00:00:30.092 --> 00:00:33.747
A timeout from an external provider means the outcome may be unknown.

00:00:33.896 --> 00:00:38.283
Query or reconcile using the same provider key before issuing another effect.

00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:42.081
Human approval does not solve duplicate delivery by itself.

00:00:42.231 --> 00:00:46.765
ApprovalLedger stores a proposal and accepts only the exact same record from

00:00:46.765 --> 00:00:48.277
the matching human context.

00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:52.121
One hundred concurrent approvals produce one simulated receipt.

00:00:52.271 --> 00:00:57.002
The ledger is in memory and does not call a payment provider,

00:00:57.002 --> 00:01:01.734
so it demonstrates identity binding and same-process idempotency, not durable refund processing.

00:01:01.884 --> 00:01:06.188
Extend the ledger with a database schema for proposal and execution states.

00:01:06.338 --> 00:01:10.713
Define how to recover after a crash between provider success and local

00:01:10.713 --> 00:01:11.443
receipt storage.

00:01:11.593 --> 00:01:14.109
Include a changed-amount attack in the tests.

00:01:14.259 --> 00:01:20.539
Persist the exact proposal, human identity, approval version, and provider idempotency key.

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:25.504
After restart, reconcile an IN_PROGRESS or UNKNOWN execution with the provider.

00:01:25.654 --> 00:01:29.818
Never create a new key merely because the previous HTTP call timed

00:01:29.818 --> 00:01:30.165
out.

00:01:30.315 --> 00:01:34.399
Eligibility checks and amount limits remain separate from approval storage.

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:35.220
Question.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.114
Why must approval expire?

00:01:37.265 --> 00:01:37.841
Answer.

00:01:37.991 --> 00:01:42.067
The business state and user intent may change; a stale approval should

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:43.425
not grant indefinite authority.

00:01:43.575 --> 00:01:44.247
Question.

00:01:44.397 --> 00:01:46.667
Does synchronized provide crash safety?

00:01:46.817 --> 00:01:47.393
Answer.

00:01:47.543 --> 00:01:48.027
No.

00:01:48.177 --> 00:01:52.279
It coordinates threads in one process and loses its in-memory state on

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:52.621
restart.
